WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 1.1.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.010
What DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.163
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.163 --> 00:00:09.724
able to describe the DeFi stack and explain

00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:11.935
why every yield is payment for a risk.

00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:14.525
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.525 --> 00:00:16.210
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.925
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:20.450
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.612
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:23.912 --> 00:00:28.281
DeFi replaces intermediaries (banks, brokers, exchanges) with

00:00:28.281 --> 00:00:31.733
smart contracts: public programs on a blockchain

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:34.852
that hold assets and follow fixed rules.

00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:37.915
The stack, bottom to top: Three properties make

00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:41.935
DeFi powerful and dangerous: Risk-first rule: before comparing

00:00:41.935 --> 00:00:45.107
returns, list what could make you lose money.

00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:47.767
A higher yield means you're being paid to carry

00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:50.837
more risk, whether or not the website mentions it.

00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:53.997
First, Blockchain: records every balance and

00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:57.246
transaction (Ethereum, L2s, other chains).

00:00:57.246 --> 00:00:59.976
Next, Smart contracts: the protocol logic

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:02.681
(a lending market, an exchange pool).

00:01:02.681 --> 00:01:05.791
Then, Tokens: the assets the contracts move.

00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:09.256
Also, Wallets: where you hold keys and sign actions.

00:01:09.256 --> 00:01:11.621
On top of that, Interfaces: websites

00:01:11.621 --> 00:01:13.966
that build transactions for you.

00:01:13.966 --> 00:01:16.681
They're a convenience, not the protocol itself.

00:01:17.982 --> 00:01:20.553
And Data layers: explorers and dashboards

00:01:20.553 --> 00:01:22.812
you use to verify what happened.

00:01:22.812 --> 00:01:25.941
Plus, Self-custody: nobody can freeze your funds,

00:01:25.941 --> 00:01:28.432
and nobody can recover them either.

00:01:28.432 --> 00:01:32.307
One more: Composability: protocols plug into each other.

00:01:32.307 --> 00:01:35.008
That makes products powerful, and a position can

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:37.512
fail because of something it depends on.

00:01:37.512 --> 00:01:41.802
And finally, Transparency: you can verify everything on-chain.

00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:43.502
Transparent doesn't mean safe.

00:01:44.802 --> 00:01:47.317
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:52.027
A pool advertises 12% A.P.Y. on a stablecoin.

00:01:52.027 --> 00:01:55.370
Before depositing, ask: If you can't answer these, you

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.222
don't know what the 12% is paying you for.

00:01:58.222 --> 00:02:00.407
Where does the 12% come from?

00:02:00.407 --> 00:02:04.332
Borrowers paying interest, trading fees, or a reward token?.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:06.707
Which stablecoin, and what backs it?

00:02:06.707 --> 00:02:08.422
(Lesson 1.5).

00:02:08.422 --> 00:02:10.058
Which contracts hold the money,

00:02:10.058 --> 00:02:11.687
and who can upgrade them?.

00:02:11.687 --> 00:02:14.232
Is there a bridge or oracle involved?.

00:02:14.232 --> 00:02:17.057
How do I withdraw, and could withdrawals be blocked?.

00:02:18.357 --> 00:02:19.817
Here's your checklist.

00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:21.352
Do it now, for real.

00:02:21.352 --> 00:02:22.869
First, I can name the six

00:02:22.869 --> 00:02:24.627
layers of the DeFi stack.

00:02:24.627 --> 00:02:28.332
Next, I can explain why self-custody cuts both ways.

00:02:28.332 --> 00:02:30.506
And finally, before any yield, I ask

00:02:30.506 --> 00:02:32.387
"what am I being paid to risk?".

00:02:33.687 --> 00:02:34.897
Question 1.

00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:37.162
Why can composability increase risk?

00:02:41.582 --> 00:02:45.006
The answer: A position inherits the failure risk of

00:02:45.006 --> 00:02:48.627
every protocol, asset, oracle and bridge it depends on.

00:02:49.927 --> 00:02:51.172
Question 2.

00:02:51.172 --> 00:02:53.577
Is a website the same thing as the protocol?

00:02:57.997 --> 00:03:01.877
The answer: No. The interface only builds transactions.

00:03:01.877 --> 00:03:04.126
The contracts are the protocol, and

00:03:04.126 --> 00:03:06.562
interfaces can be faked or compromised.

00:03:07.862 --> 00:03:09.132
Question 3.

00:03:09.132 --> 00:03:11.837
What is the risk-first mindset in one sentence?

00:03:16.257 --> 00:03:18.659
The answer: Identify what could make you

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:20.886
lose money before you compare returns.

00:03:22.186 --> 00:03:23.431
Let's recap.

00:03:23.431 --> 00:03:26.105
Blockchain: records every balance and

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:29.361
transaction (Ethereum, L2s, other chains).

00:03:29.361 --> 00:03:31.877
Smart contracts: the protocol logic (a

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:34.371
lending market, an exchange pool).

00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:37.001
Tokens: the assets the contracts move.

00:03:37.001 --> 00:03:39.456
Wallets: where you hold keys and sign actions.

00:03:40.756 --> 00:03:43.271
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:43.271 --> 00:03:47.303
Next up, Lesson 1.2: How a transaction actually happens.
